# Logging instructions for this project

This file tells you, the coding agent, how to log, and what your code must
check while it runs. Read it before you write or change any code, and follow
it in every file you touch. Everything you need is in this file.

The test that decides whether logging is good enough: a person who has never
seen the code must be able to read the records and retell the whole story of
any single piece of work from them alone — what it was trying to do, what
each step did, what data went in and came out, how it ended, and why, if it
failed. If a record cannot be understood without opening the source, it has
failed this test.

## 1. Every piece of work gets an ID you can follow

A piece of work is one request, one job, one run. The code calls it an
**intent**.

- When a piece of work starts, it gets an **ID**: a random string of 32
  characters. Every record made during that work carries the ID. To read the
  story of one piece of work, take the records that carry its ID and read
  them in order.
- Each step inside the work gets its own smaller ID: 16 characters, shared by
  all the records that step produces.
- Every record also carries a **number** counting its position in that piece
  of work (the field is called `seq`). Timestamps can tie or jump; the number
  cannot. When the numbers are in order, you are reading what happened in
  order.
- If a piece of work starts work of its own — a job that splits into per-item
  jobs, a follow-up run — the new work gets its own ID, and it names the ID
  of the work that started it. That is how a story continues across
  boundaries.

Everything is written as one JSON object per line, in one stream. Nothing is
kept anywhere else.

## 2. What every record carries

A record is one line in the log — one JSON object. Every record carries:

- when it happened, and how important it is (its **level**);
- which piece of work it belongs to, which step made it, and its number in
  that work's order (the IDs and number from section 1);
- an **event name**: what kind of thing happened (section 3);
- a **message**: one plain sentence saying what happened. The message is for
  the person reading the line; the fields are for filtering and checking;
- the **facts**: the data that moved (what went out, what came back), the
  results, and the details of any check (section 7);
- when something failed: how it failed, what happened because of it, and the
  error's details (section 6).

Two things are never true of a good record set: a record that belongs to no
piece of work, and a step that does real work but leaves no records.

## 3. Name each event from what the step is doing

You choose the event name yourself, at the place in the code where it is
written, from the purpose of that step — the thing it exists to do. Start
from the top: what the system is for, what this operation is for, what parts
the work has, what can happen to each part, and only then choose the name.

Do not keep a fixed list of names to pick from. A fixed list forces every
situation into labels that don't fit it, and a label that doesn't describe
the actual situation tells the reader nothing.

Shape every name the same way, so all names stay readable and predictable:

- lowercase words joined by dots: `orders.fetch.started`;
- the name describes the step, the same way everywhere it appears;
- nothing that changes goes in the name — IDs, counts, and values live in
  fields. If the name changes every time, records of the same kind can no
  longer be found together;
- the start and the end of a step say so: `.started`, `.succeeded`,
  `.failed`;
- mark the step itself in `domain:verb` form: `orders:fetch` — domain
  `orders`, verb `fetch`.

## 4. Choose the level by impact

How important a record is depends on purpose — what the code was trying to
do. A payment failing is a real problem; a statistics ping failing is nearly
nothing; a user cancelling is no problem at all. Write each record so its
situation is clear enough that only one reading is possible.

Levels:

- **fatal** — the program cannot continue (bad setup at startup,
  unrecoverable state). The program must stop.
- **error** — something unexpected happened; the work or the caller has to
  deal with it.
- **warn** — a problem that was expected and is being handled. Giving up
  after retries goes here: one record when the retries are used up, never one
  per attempt.
- **debug** — not a problem: a cancelled request, work skipped on purpose.

## 5. Start and finish every step

- When a piece of work begins, write an entry record: what it wants, and the
  inputs it starts with.
- Before a real step (a call, a store write, a job), write that the step
  started. After it, write exactly one ending: succeeded, with the result,
  or failed, with the details. Never skip the success record — it is half the
  story. Without it, a reader cannot tell finished from hung.
- Write down the data that moved: what the step sent, what it received back,
  what it produced. The real content — not a summary like "error occurred",
  not a pointer like "see file X". The content is how the reader sees it
  working, or sees the exact error.
- If something happens inside a step that the reader needs to know — one item
  of a batch failed, a retry happened — write an extra record from inside
  that step.

## 6. When something goes wrong — four cases

1. **The code throws.** An error is raised and comes out of the step.
2. **The answer says it failed.** The call itself worked — the connection
   succeeded, a response arrived — but the content inside says it failed.
   Example: a 200 OK whose body carries an error. The transport succeeding is
   not the point; whether the purpose was achieved is. Write it as a failure,
   with the error content from the body.
3. **The data is wrong.** Nothing threw and nothing reported an error, but
   the data is not what it must be: a field missing, a number where text was
   expected, a value out of range. This is a failure too — the most dangerous
   kind, because the code will carry on with bad data if it isn't caught.
   Write it with what was expected and what was found (section 7).
4. **Not every bad-looking thing is a failure.** A user cancelling, an
   expected branch, "nothing yet, keep waiting" — these are normal. Write
   them as normal records (debug or info), never as errors.

When you write a failure, the record says: what the step was trying to do,
what was expected, what was found, whether it was handled or stopped the
work, and the error's details.

Name the kind: `failureClass` is `thrown`, `returned`, or `shape` — one for
each of the first three cases above. Say what happened because of it:
`consequence` is `handled` (dealt with here), `escaped` (left the step for
the caller), or `terminal` (the work stopped).

Write the error's details out as plain fields — its name, message, where it
was raised, its code, and anything else it carries. Never put the raw error
object in a record.

Log each failure once, where it happened. When it travels further up through
the code, do not write it again at every level — re-writing the same failure
makes a small problem look like an avalanche. And never catch an error and
silently continue: log it and pass it on, or handle it and record that you
did.

## 7. Check the data — logging is only half the job

Writing down what happened is not enough. The values your code handles must
also be checked: where they enter (environment, configuration, dependencies —
before any real work starts), where they move (before a request goes out,
when a response comes back), and where they leave (before a result is
returned or stored). A value that is wrong must be caught at the step that
produced it, never carried on.

When you check something, write four things into the record:

- **expected** — what it should be;
- **actual** — what it was;
- **where** — which value, at which point;
- **verdict** — "clean" or "violation".

A check that comes back "violation" is a failure record, at error level, even
though nothing threw and every request returned success.

This changes what "succeeded" means: a step has succeeded only when it
finished AND its result was checked. Write the success record after the check
passes — not before, and not in place of it.

Check the setup at startup too: environment, configuration, dependencies,
present and valid. If the setup is wrong, the program cannot do its work —
record it as fatal and stop.

## 8. Secrets: shape, never values

Never write the value of a secret into a record — not tokens, passwords,
keys, cookies, or authorization headers. The value is exactly what must not
be in the log.

Instead, write the shape: that it is present, how long it is, what fields it
has. `hasToken` (true), `tokenLength` (42), `dataKeys` (the field names). One
field name, one meaning.

Before logging any request headers, remove the authorization and cookie
headers. If the removal fails, drop the data entirely and write a fixed line
that says so, with no data in it.

IDs, usernames, and resource names are fine to write.

## 9. Stores, waiting, and crashes

- **Stores (database, cache, file).** Write the key and the field names
  involved, never the stored values. "Not found" is its own kind of error,
  not an empty result. When the store fails, write the failure and pass it
  on.
- **Waiting (jobs, polling).** "Nothing yet, keep waiting" is normal — do not
  write it as an error. When the job finally fails, that is a normal failure
  record.
- **Crashes.** Handle the two top-level events: an uncaught error, and a
  rejected promise nobody handled. Write the full error details in the
  record; give the outside world a short, safe message with no internals.
  Before the program exits, flush the log — write out anything still waiting
  — so the crash itself stays in the record.

## 10. The shared module — create it once, import it everywhere

Every project has one logging module. Create the file below once, exactly as
given, saved as `logger.js` (install the logging library with `npm i pino`
once). Every other file imports what it needs from this module. Never call
the logging library directly anywhere else. Never use `console.log` or
`console.error` at all.

```js
// logger.js — the shared logging module for this project.
// One shared logger; use log.child(...) to attach fixed facts once.
// Records carry the ID of the piece of work (traceId), the ID of the step
// (spanId), and their position in the work (seq). Errors are written out
// as plain fields with serializeError. Secrets are written as shape, never
// as values. Values are checked with validate().

import { randomBytes } from "node:crypto";
import pino from "pino";

// --- 1. The one shared logger ----------------------------------------------

export function createLogger(options = {}) {
  return pino({
    level: process.env.LOG_LEVEL || "info",
    messageKey: "message",
    timestamp: pino.stdTimeFunctions.isoTime,
    base: {
      service: process.env.SERVICE_NAME || "app",
      version: process.env.SERVICE_VERSION || "0.0.0",
    },
    formatters: { level: (label) => ({ severity: label.toUpperCase() }) },
    ...options,
  });
}

// The one shared instance. Import `log` everywhere; do not call pino() again.
export const log = createLogger();

// --- 2. Error serialization -------------------------------------------------

// Every caught error goes through this before it is logged: it turns the
// error into plain fields. Never log a raw Error object.
export function serializeError(err, context = {}) {
  if (err == null || typeof err !== "object") {
    return { message: String(err), ...context };
  }
  const out = {
    name: err.name,
    message: err.message,
    stack: err.stack,
    ...context,
  };
  for (const k of ["code", "status", "statusText", "cause", "body"]) {
    if (err[k] !== undefined) out[k] = err[k];
  }
  return out;
}

// --- 3. Redaction: shape, never values --------------------------------------

// Write sizes and shapes, never values. Name each field after what it holds:
// hasToken (true/false), tokenLength (a number), dataKeys (a list). One
// field name, one meaning.
export function presence(value) {
  if (value == null || value === "") return false;
  if (typeof value === "string" || Array.isArray(value)) return value.length;
  if (typeof value === "object") return Object.keys(value).length;
  return Boolean(value);
}

const SENSITIVE_HEADERS = new Set([
  "authorization",
  "cookie",
  "set-cookie",
  "proxy-authorization",
]);

// Remove auth material from headers before they are logged. If anything goes
// wrong while removing, return no headers at all and let the caller drop the
// data — never fall through with the raw headers.
export function redactHeaders(headers = {}) {
  try {
    const kept = {};
    const stripped = [];
    for (const [k, v] of Object.entries(headers)) {
      if (SENSITIVE_HEADERS.has(String(k).toLowerCase())) {
        stripped.push(String(k).toLowerCase());
        continue;
      }
      kept[k] = v;
    }
    return { kept, stripped };
  } catch {
    return { kept: null, stripped: null, redactionFailed: true };
  }
}

// --- 4. Checking values -----------------------------------------------------
//
// validate(expected, actual, where) -> { verdict: "clean" }
//                                   | { verdict: "violation", violations: [...] }
//
// The expected shape is written at the call site — it belongs to that call,
// not to a central list. Shape language, per field:
//   "string" | "number" | "boolean" | "object" | "array"   — a bare type
//   { type, length?, min?, max?, oneOf?, of?, shape? }     — with detail
//     length  exact length (strings, arrays)
//     min/max numeric bounds
//     oneOf   allowed values
//     of      element spec for arrays
//     shape   nested fields for objects
export function validate(expected, actual, where = "value") {
  const violations = [];
  checkFields(expected, actual, where, "", violations);
  return violations.length === 0
    ? { verdict: "clean" }
    : { verdict: "violation", violations };
}

function checkFields(expected, actual, where, path, violations) {
  for (const [field, spec] of Object.entries(expected)) {
    const value = actual == null ? undefined : actual[field];
    const at = path ? `${path}.${field}` : field;
    checkSpec(spec, value, where, at, violations);
  }
}

function checkSpec(spec, value, where, at, violations) {
  if (typeof spec === "string") {
    if (typeOf(value) !== spec) pushViolation(violations, where, at, spec, value);
    return;
  }
  if (spec.type && typeOf(value) !== spec.type) {
    pushViolation(violations, where, at, describeExpected(spec), value);
    return;
  }
  if (spec.length != null && (value == null || value.length !== spec.length)) {
    pushViolation(violations, where, at, describeExpected(spec), value);
  }
  if (spec.min != null && !(typeof value === "number" && value >= spec.min)) {
    pushViolation(violations, where, at, describeExpected(spec), value);
  }
  if (spec.max != null && !(typeof value === "number" && value <= spec.max)) {
    pushViolation(violations, where, at, describeExpected(spec), value);
  }
  if (spec.oneOf && !spec.oneOf.includes(value)) {
    pushViolation(violations, where, at, describeExpected(spec), value);
  }
  if (spec.of != null && Array.isArray(value)) {
    value.forEach((item, i) => checkSpec(spec.of, item, where, `${at}[${i}]`, violations));
  }
  if (spec.shape && typeOf(value) === "object") {
    checkFields(spec.shape, value, where, at, violations);
  }
}

function pushViolation(violations, where, field, expected, value) {
  violations.push({ where, field, expected, actual: describe(value) });
}

function describeExpected(spec) {
  if (typeof spec === "string") return spec;
  let d = spec.type || "value";
  if (spec.length != null) d += `(${spec.length})`;
  if (spec.min != null || spec.max != null) {
    d += ` ${spec.min != null ? `>= ${spec.min}` : ""}${spec.min != null && spec.max != null ? ", " : ""}${spec.max != null ? `<= ${spec.max}` : ""}`;
  }
  if (spec.oneOf) d += ` one of ${JSON.stringify(spec.oneOf)}`;
  return d;
}

function typeOf(v) {
  if (v === null) return "null";
  if (Array.isArray(v)) return "array";
  return typeof v;
}

function describe(value) {
  if (value === undefined) return "undefined";
  if (value === null) return "null";
  if (typeof value === "string") {
    return `string(${value.length})${value.length <= 48 ? ` "${value}"` : ""}`;
  }
  if (Array.isArray(value)) return `array(${value.length})`;
  if (typeof value === "object") return `object(${Object.keys(value).length} keys)`;
  return `${typeof value} ${String(value)}`;
}

// --- 5. Pieces of work ------------------------------------------------------
//
// startIntent() opens one piece of work and returns handles for it:
//   intent.started(inputs, message)      the entry record: what it wants
//   intent.step({ step, event })         one real step of the work:
//       .started() / .succeeded() / .failed()   the beginning and one ending
//       .note(...)                              extra records inside the step
//   intent.flow(level, event, fields, msg)      one-off records (a retry, a
//                                               cancel) for the work itself
//   intent.succeeded(fields, message)    the work finished, with its result
//   intent.failed({ err, ... })          the work failed; pass `cause` to
//                                        point at the record that caused it
//
// Work that starts its own work opens its own intent and passes the starter's
// ID: startIntent({ ..., parentTraceId: parent.traceId })
export function startIntent({ logger = log, subject, goal, parentTraceId } = {}) {
  const traceId = randomBytes(16).toString("hex");
  const rootSpanId = randomBytes(8).toString("hex");
  const openedAt = Date.now();
  const base = { traceId, ...(parentTraceId ? { parentTraceId } : {}) };
  const intentLog = logger.child(base);
  let seq = 0;
  const emit = (level, fields, message) => {
    seq += 1;
    intentLog[level]({ ...fields, seq }, message);
    return seq;
  };

  return {
    traceId,

    started(inputs = {}, message = goal || subject) {
      return emit("info", { spanId: rootSpanId, event: `${subject}.started`, ...inputs }, message);
    },

    flow(level, event, fields = {}, message) {
      return emit(level, { spanId: rootSpanId, event, ...fields }, message);
    },

    step({ step, event }) {
      const spanId = randomBytes(8).toString("hex");
      const t0 = Date.now();
      return {
        spanId,
        note(level, eventName, fields = {}, message) {
          return emit(level, { spanId, step, event: eventName, ...fields }, message);
        },
        started(fields = {}, message = `${step} started`) {
          return emit("info", { spanId, step, event: `${event}.started`, ...fields }, message);
        },
        succeeded(fields = {}, message = `${step} succeeded`, { level = "info" } = {}) {
          return emit(
            level,
            { spanId, step, event: `${event}.succeeded`, outcome: "success", durationMs: Date.now() - t0, ...fields },
            message,
          );
        },
        failed({ err, failureClass, consequence = "handled", fields = {}, message, level = "error" }) {
          return emit(
            level,
            {
              spanId,
              step,
              event: `${event}.failed`,
              outcome: "failure",
              failureClass,
              consequence,
              durationMs: Date.now() - t0,
              err: serializeError(err),
              ...fields,
            },
            message || `${step} failed`,
          );
        },
      };
    },

    succeeded(fields = {}, message = `${subject} succeeded`, { level = "info" } = {}) {
      return emit(
        level,
        { spanId: rootSpanId, event: `${subject}.succeeded`, outcome: "success", durationMs: Date.now() - openedAt, ...fields },
        message,
      );
    },

    failed({ err, failureClass = "thrown", consequence = "terminal", cause, fields = {}, message, level = "error" }) {
      return emit(
        level,
        {
          spanId: rootSpanId,
          event: `${subject}.failed`,
          outcome: "failure",
          failureClass,
          consequence,
          ...(cause ? { cause } : {}),
          durationMs: Date.now() - openedAt,
          err: serializeError(err),
          ...fields,
        },
        message || `${subject} failed`,
      );
    },
  };
}
```

How to use it:

- In each file, make one logger that carries the file's fixed facts, once:
  `const moduleLog = log.child({ component: "orders", function: "runImport" })`.
  `log.child(...)` makes a copy of the shared logger with those facts
  attached; every record it writes carries them, so they are never repeated
  per call. (`component` is the module name, lowercase with dashes where
  needed; `function` is the function's name.)
- Open a piece of work with `startIntent`, record its steps with
  `intent.step`, and close it with `intent.succeeded` or `intent.failed`.
- The full pattern, one step from beginning to end:

```js
import { log, startIntent, presence, redactHeaders, validate } from "./logger.js";

const moduleLog = log.child({ component: "orders", function: "runImport" });

const intent = startIntent({ logger: moduleLog, subject: "orders.import", goal: `Import orders for ${date}` });
intent.started({ source, date, apiKeyLength: presence(apiKey) });

const step = intent.step({ step: "orders:fetch", event: "orders.fetch" });
step.started({ request: { url, headers: redactHeaders(headers).kept } });
try {
  const body = await callSupplier();
  const shape = validate(RESPONSE_SHAPE, body, "orders.fetch response");
  if (shape.verdict === "violation") {
    throw Object.assign(new Error("response shape violated"), { code: "BAD_RESPONSE_SHAPE" });
  }
  step.succeeded({
    rows: body.orders.length,
    response: body,                        // what came back, in full
    expected: RESPONSE_SHAPE,              // the check, written out
    actual: { rows: body.orders.length },
    where: "orders.fetch response",
    verdict: shape.verdict,
  });
} catch (err) {
  const seq = step.failed({ err, failureClass: "thrown", consequence: "terminal" });
  intent.failed({ err, failureClass: "thrown", consequence: "terminal",
    cause: { traceId: intent.traceId, seq } });
  return;
}
intent.succeeded({ imported: body.orders.length });
```

