Reading it cold: the import wanted the day's orders from the supplier, and it
had a key. It fetched them, removed the auth header before writing the
request down, and checked the response against its expected shape — clean. It
checked each order: the second had a number where a 36-character string was
required, so it was set aside as a failure, handled without stopping the
work, and the step completed with its counts. It saved the two valid orders
(the store records carry key names, never values) and checked the result of
the save. It ended: 2 imported, 1 set aside, 63 milliseconds — the whole
story in nine records, all carrying the same work ID, in order, with nothing
that needs the source code to understand.

## 12. Never do this

- Never write to the console (`console.log`, `console.error`). Use the shared
  logger.
- Never create a second logger or call the logging library directly.
- Never write a record without context.
- Never put a raw error object in a record — write its details out first.
- Never catch an error, log it, and continue as if nothing happened.
- Never write a normal event (a cancel, an expected branch) as an error.
- Never write a secret value into a record.
- Never write a failure without saying what kind it was and what happened
  because of it.
- Never put changing values (IDs, counts) into event names.
- Never write the same failure again at every level it travels through.
- Never write a pointer ("saved to file X", "see record 12") instead of the
  data itself.
- Never skip the success record after a step that succeeded.
- Never repeat fixed fields on every call — attach them once.
- Never leave a step that can fail without a record, or a value that was
  never checked.

## 13. Before you call the work done

1. All logging goes through the shared module. No console writes, no second
   logger.
2. Each file attaches its fixed facts once; each piece of work attaches its
   ID once.
3. Every piece of work opens with a record saying what it wants and its
   inputs.
4. Every step has a beginning record and exactly one ending; endings carry
   the result; success records exist.
5. Every record carries the work ID, the step ID, and its number.
6. Every caught error is written out, logged where it happened, then passed
   on or handled. Nothing is swallowed.
7. Failures say how they failed and what happened because of them; a failure
   that came from an earlier one points back at it.
8. Every check is recorded with expected / actual / where / verdict; a
   violated check is a failure record.
9. No secret values appear anywhere; headers are cleaned before logging; if
   cleaning fails, the data is dropped.
10. Levels are chosen by impact: one warn when retries are used up, a cancel
    as debug, a broken setup as fatal.
11. Event names follow the shape rules; the same field carries the same
    meaning everywhere.
12. The test on the first page passes: read one piece of work's records cold,
    and you can tell its whole story.
